/* MARK: - Help */

"help.title" = "MenuBarKeeper · How to use";
"help.status.collapsed" = "Now: %d apps hidden";
"help.status.expanded" = "Now: everything visible";
"help.mechanism.unavailable" = "Hiding engine: unavailable — %@";
"help.mechanism.available" = "Hiding engine: available";
"help.mechanism.lastError" = " (last request rejected: %@)";
"help.body" = "What it does\nGroups menu bar icons by app. Apps you select are moved into a hidden area — their icons leave the menu bar and come back on demand.\n\nGetting started\n1. Grant Accessibility permission on first launch. It is used only to identify which app owns each icon. Nothing is recorded or uploaded.\n2. Click any row to select an app. Its icons disappear from the menu bar immediately, and your selection is remembered.\n3. Left-click the menu bar icon to reveal the hidden icons just below it; click again to put them back.\n4. On the floating bar:\n   · click an icon — switch to that app\n   · right-click an icon — open, preferences, restore to menu bar, hide, quit, force quit\n   · click the arrow — show or hide the menu bar (the same button toggles both ways)\n   · click the MenuBarKeeper icon — open the main window\n5. Turn on \"Hide on launch\" to apply your selection automatically at startup.\n\nKeyboard shortcuts (work even while icons are hidden)\n⌥⌘M   reveal or hide the floating bar\n⌥⌘\\   hide or show menu bar icons\n\nThree notes\n· Hidden apps keep running. Only the icon is hidden, nothing else changes.\n· Once an icon is hidden, macOS no longer exposes it, so the app's own menu is out of reach. Use \"Restore to menu bar\" on the floating bar to bring a single icon back and use its menu normally.\n· System items (clock, battery, Control Center) are never hidden.";

